# Board #78: an LED's label, edited and saved.
#
# The LED is the family witness. Before #78 it carried a LABEL attribute and no text field at
# all, so its label neither drew nor edited; it now conforms to InstanceLabelProvider with
# `computeLabelTextField(AVOID_LEFT)` -- for the default west-facing LED placed at (300, 200),
# bounds (300,190) 20x20, that puts an EAST label at (322, 200) H_LEFT/V_CENTER and a NORTH one
# at (310, 188) H_CENTER/V_BOTTOM.
#
# ── WHAT THIS SCRIPT IS NOT, AND WHY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It was asked to click the LED's label, retype it and commit with Return. It cannot, and the
# obstacle is in the gate rather than in either editor. Two independent blockers, both measured:
#
#  1. **There is no `type` op.** The script language has exactly seven operations and `key`
#     accepts only `delete` and `backspace` (EditBridge.java:333-336,
#     EditParityTests.swift:210-211). Nothing in either driver can deliver a character to an open
#     caret, so no script can retype anything.
#
#  2. **`tool text` + `click` throws in the harness.** Adding those two lines here produced, from
#     `--regenerate`:
#         FAIL 12-label-led.script: NullPointerException: Cannot invoke
#         "java.awt.Graphics.getFontMetrics(java.awt.Font)" because "g" is null
#     `InstanceTextField.getTextCaret` (InstanceTextField.java:100-119) measures through
#     `event.getCanvas().getGraphics()`, NOT through the `Graphics` the tool was handed, and an
#     unrealized `Canvas` JPanel returns null from it. EditBridge's own BufferedImage `Graphics`
#     (EditBridge.java:231) never reaches that call. So the text tool is listed in the script
#     language and is unusable for every component that has a text field.
#
# And even with both fixed, a byte-comparison of the saved .circ could not witness *where* the
# caret opened: the file records the label's text, font and `labelloc`, never its computed pixel
# position, and `TextTool.editingStopped` (TextTool.java:78-120) makes a hit and a miss write the
# same bytes -- a hit commits old-text == new-text, a miss creates a blank Text that `isEmpty`
# sends down the `a = null` arm. Only a `type` op makes the two outcomes differ.
#
# ── WHAT IT DOES GATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The half that is reachable, and that is genuinely new: both `label` and `labelloc` edited on
# an LED through the real attribute-table model (EditBridgeAttrTable -> SetAttributeAction, the
# same path the GUI's table uses), then saved. `labelloc` is the attribute `labelPlacement`
# reads, so this pins the input to the new placement even though the output is not in the file.
# The second `setattr` also stops the two actions from merging, as 06-set-attribute does.

tool	add:LED
click	300	200
setattr	300	200	label	Q
setattr	300	200	labelloc	north
